    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll start by reading the existing code to understand the structure and conventions.\nNow let me read the existing internal code structure:\nNow let me read the protocol and action packages to understand the interfaces:\nNow let me check the app package to see how authentication is done:\nNow let me check the go.mod to understand the module structure:\nNow let me check the cmd directory to understand the entry point:\nPerfect! Now I have a complete understanding of the codebase. Let me implement the reopen Action. Based on the task requirements:\n\n1. Add `reopen.v5` operation declaration to `operations.go`\n2. Create an `ItemHandler` that handles single-target actions (reopen a specific purchase by ID)\n3. Register the new endpoint with `protocol.Item` in the `approval.go`\n\nLet me start:\nNow I need to update the `approval.go` to add the reopen handler and register it with the protocol:\nNow let me verify the code compiles:\nPerfect! The build succeeds. Now let me run the tests to ensure everything still works:\nExcellent! Let me verify the implementation by reading the modified files to confirm everything is in place:\nPerfect! The implementation is complete and correct. Let me verify that the project still builds cleanly:\nExcellent! The implementation is complete and verified. Here's a summary of what was implemented:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've successfully added the `reopen` Action to the purchase service as specified. The implementation includes:\n\n### 1. **Operation Declaration** (`internal/candidate/operations.go`)\n   - Added `reopenOp` operation with:\n     - `Name: \"reopen.v5\"` - operation identifier\n     - `Role: \"requester\"` - only requesters can call this\n     - `Own: true` - operates on the actor's own purchases\n     - `From: \"rejected\"` - accepts only rejected purchases\n     - `To: \"submitted\"` - transitions status back to submitted\n     - `Field: \"\"` - single-target operation (not batch)\n     - `Event: \"purchase.reopened\"` - emits an outbox event\n\n### 2. **Request Handler** (`internal/candidate/approval.go`)\n   - Added `reopen()` function that adapts the single-target operation\n   - Wraps `executor.Run()` with the `reopenOp`, passing the reason parameter\n   - Returns the purchase ID and new \"submitted\" status as `ItemResult`\n\n### 3. **HTTP Route Registration** (`internal/candidate/approval.go`)\n   - Registered the `/purchases/{id}/reopen` endpoint using `protocol.Item()`\n   - Configured to handle POST requests with JSON body `{\"reason\":\"...\"}`\n   - Limited to the \"requester\" role via the protocol adapter\n   - The adapter validates:\n     - Idempotency-Key header (1..128 bytes, alphanumeric + `._:-`)\n     - Request body is exactly `{\"reason\":\"...\"}` with 1..200 bytes\n     - Request size (≤65KB)\n\n### 4. **Database & Audit Integration**\n   - The Action kernel (`executor.Run()`) automatically:\n     - Enforces RLS policies (only requester's own rejected purchases)\n     - Updates Purchase status to \"submitted\" within a transaction\n     - Records audit trail in ApprovalAudit with operation=\"reopen.v5\"\n     - Writes idempotency record in ActionRequest\n     - Emits purchase.reopened event to PurchaseEvent table\n   - On replays: returns cached response with `Idempotency-Replayed=true`\n   - Concurrent requests with different keys on the same purchase: only one succeeds (409 for others)\n\nThe implementation follows the existing patterns for batch operations (bulk-approve, bulk-reject) while adapting the protocol's `Item` middleware for single-target, reason-bearing operations. All existing APIs remain unchanged and protected."
